  **(1) PHP closure example with `wp_interactivity_get_context()`**

  Under "Derived state" > "Initializing the derived state", the doc says: "In some cases, when the derived state depends on the local context and the local context can change dynamically in the server, instead of the initial derived state, you can use a function (Closure) that calculates it dynamically." The example is:

  ```php
  <?php
  wp_interactivity_state( 'myProductPlugin', array(
    'list'    => array( 1, 2, 3 ),
    'factor'  => 3,
    'product' => function() {
      $state   = wp_interactivity_state();
      $context = wp_interactivity_get_context();
      return $context['item'] * $state['factor'];
    }
  ));
  ?>

  <template
    data-wp-interactive="myProductPlugin"
    data-wp-each="state.list"
  >
    <span data-wp-text="state.product"></span>
  </template>
  ```

  The doc says this renders `<span>3</span>`, `<span>6</span>`, `<span>9</span>` (directives omitted).

